A||||6||Shaun41178||Bought Power Pulley, now have ??'s
Z||000000||Shaun41178||08-01-99||02:29 AM||||I just receieved my power pulley from the Fiero store yesterday.  I was taking a look at it and the bolts that they sens with the pulley are not the same as the bolts that hold the stock pulley on.  The bolts with the power pulley(PP) look like they have to be tightened with a allen wrench or some tool like an allen wrench.  Basically you can not put a socket over the bolt and tighten it down cause there are no sides to grip with the socket.<p>Is it still possible to use the stock bolts with this pulley or do I have to use the bolts that came with the pp?  If I can't use the stock bolts what kind of tool do I use to make sure that they get tight enough to where they won't come out since I can't get a torque wrench on it?<p>Any help from people (pretender) that have bought and installed a  pp would be greatly appreciated.  Tips please.  Thanks
Z||000001||batboy||08-01-99||07:34 AM||||I've never installed a power pulley, but those new bolts sound like they might be torque bit bolts. Do they sort of look like a allen head except the recessed part looks slightly star shaped? If so, you can buy torque bit sockets, I have a set of them that I bought at Sears. Torque bit bolts are used in many places on the Fiero, for example the caliper slider bolts and the seat belt retainer bolts. Even if they are allen head bolts, you can buy sockets for them too. That way you can use your torque wrench to tighten them down. Hope this helps you find the right tools. It's kind of hard to tell exactly what they are without seeing them first.
Z||000002||theogre||08-01-99||02:32 PM||||Torx(TM) bolts........ Don't use an allen key in them... take them to the parts/tool store, and get the right bit for them.<p>You could likely use the stock bolts, but the new ones are stronger. Replacing old bolts for such things is always a good idea where posible.<p>Bat, You have Torx bolts in the calipers? what year is it? Mine are the standard 1/4 inch allen type head, and mines an 87.....
Z||000003||batboy||08-01-99||03:13 PM||||After I posted my comments, I realized that I had mispelled Torx (not torque), but I was hoping nobody else would notice (oops). Ogre, both of my Fieros have Torx caliper slider bolts. One is a 1987 SE and the other is my 1985 batmobile. I'd be interested in hearing what some of the other guys have in their cars for caliper bolts, I had assumed that the Torx bolt was standard issue. Maybe the factory stuck in whatever they had on hand at the time.
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||08-01-99||04:31 PM||||My 88 has #55 Torx bolts in the calipers. I think my 86 did too.
Z||000005||Pretender||08-01-99||10:57 PM||||when i installed mine i had no problem..just switch to an allen head socket for your wrench..i think you can use the same bolts but these are newer and probably stronger...with the pully and the ads chip i lost 4 tenths off of my 1/4 mile times!....
Z||000006||DJRice||08-02-99||07:16 AM||||My 84 and 85 use a T-50.  I have yet to do a brake job on my 87, but it needs it.
